Date: Thu, 04 Nov 93 23:24:49 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: Future Science

Tim May says:

> Evolution sent seismic waves throughout nearly all branches of
> knowledge, causing basis vectors to realign and alter. Every field had
> to at least act itself "Are we affected by this new idea?" Some
> fields, like economics, were of course dramatically affected.

There is strong evidence (according to Stephen J Gould) that Darwin was
inspired very much by Adam Smith's ideas on individual agents competing
with each other in his Wealth of Nations.  Darwin arrived at his theory
of natural selection and the origin of species as the result of
competition between individuals within species very much as a result of
the influence of classical liberal economics.  The story that Darwin put
around about being influenced Eureka! style by Malthus was not the whole
truth.  Darwin arrived at his theory over a number of years, after
extensive reading of economists, as well as naturalists.  Early liberal
economics provided the inspiration for biology.

Gould considers that Adam Smith's ideas to have failed in economics, but
to be successful in biology.  Half right.  Even though Gould is a
socialist his essays on natural history are extremely interesting and
enjoyable.  I would especially recommend _Bully for Brontosaurus_.  Not
that there's anything especially extropian about them, except a general
broadening of horizons, unless you have an interest in biology (or
perhaps you're just a dino-nut).

