   *************************************************************

First, some important information:  The unveiling of the model will
be done somewhat later than Dec. 18.  An exact date and location will
be posted shortly.  We are currently finalizing the date with our
architect.

Second, the following is from a future issue of Chain Breaker, which
is on the presses as I write this.

PRIVATE SECTOR PUBLIC RECORDS
By Daniel Lawrence

"This DMV is too crowded. Let's go to Frank's across town." How many
of us have wished we could say that? Public record keeping is one of
the most frustrating of all government monopolies, and one of the
least necessary.
Long before there was ever a DMV, churches kept records of births,
deaths, marriages, and other events, if for no other reason than
priests were often the only ones in town who could read. When church
authority gave way to secular governments, those governments kept
records so that they could keep better track of what their subjects
were up to and what taxes they owed.
The proposed government of Oceania has no reason to keep track of its
citizens. It cannot own property, it cannot levy taxes, it cannot
issue identification, and it cannot issue licenses to work at any
trade. Why, then, would an Oceanian want such things?
Because even those who value individual freedom must live among
others. Cooperation and trade often require such services. Take, for
example, registration of births. Parents may wish to have the event
recorded by an independent agency for the protection of themselves
and the child. If the child is kidnapped, for example, such
registration may aid detectives in finding and identifying the child.
In the case of the parents' untimely death, such registration may
help the child claim inheritance or other survivor's rights.
By making such services independent of government, their consumers
will be able to choose among competitors fighting for their business.
Such companies will be able to offer a range of services to their
customers rather than one standard set. For example, a birth
registration service may offer an inexpensive registration by selling
its list of new parents to the manufacturers of child care products.
Parents who did not want to be solicited by these companies could pay
a slightly higher fee to ensure their privacy.
The recording of contracts will be an especially important function
of these agencies. Companies will be able to protect themselves from
contract disputes by registering the full text of their agreements
with reliable agencies. Contracts may also specify private courts or
arbitrators to resolve disputes that do arise. Marriage, being a
private contract in Oceania, would also be handled by these agencies,
though it is likely that most churches will handle these functions
along with the religious ceremony. Private businesses may pop up to
offer secular ceremonies along with contract recording, as is done by
the numerous wedding chapels in Las Vegas. "Photos, flowers, music,
blood tests, pre-nuptial negotiationall at Howard's one-stop wedding
shop." The blood tests, of course, would be optional.
Some agencies will probably offer general-purpose identification.
Many businesses, particularly banks, may require such identification
of their customers before granting credit or other services.
Customers could protect themselves from forgeries by registering
their signatures or private codes with such agencies. The reliability
of such identification is based entirely on the reputation of the
agency that issues it, and will be subject to market pressures.
Agencies caught issuing false IDs will quickly go out of business,
unlike government agencies.
Other forms of registration that would be popular include copyrights
and patents, where independent records of creation will be useful in
settling disputes. Colleges and vocational training centers will
register their graduates with agencies that provide lists to
employers. Such employment agencies will likely be in high demand in
the growing economy of Oceania.
Licensing is an area that will also be taken over by the private
sector in Oceania. Just as the independent Better Business Bureau
keeps records on businesses with complaints, businesses will be
created in Oceania to rate doctors, lawyers, drivers, teachers, even
other registration and licensing services! Oceanians will be free to
consult an unlicensed doctor if they wish, but most will probably
check with their favorite "Better Healers Bureau" before they go.
Driver's licenses will probably be required by most private roads.
Because the roads will be owned by individuals who have an interest
in their safety (drivers will avoid roads with dangerous
reputations), those individuals will require some form of licensing.
They may accept any of a number of competing licenses, or they may
issue their their own, but whatever they choose, it will be what
their customers demand or they won't be collecting as much in tolls
as will alternate roads with more easygoing rules. Those of you who
might fear that private road owners would create as much red tape and
hassle as our own DMV should remember that there are no laws
preventing anyone from building a competing road or other form of
transportation, so road owners will always be looking over their
shoulders and trying to stay ahead of the competition by offering a
better product. I suspect that even the most cautious of road owners
will not require the pointless violations of privacy required by the
DMVs of several states in the US. Of what use to a road owner is a
tax ID?
Private licensure of doctors may sound a little scary to some of you.
But just how well do you trust the current system? Perhaps the school
your doctor went to was accredited because it gave a generous
donation to the AMA. Perhaps the doctor's father has a golf buddy on
the local certification board who pushed the paperwork through. No
agency, privately or publically run, can completely ensure the
competence of those it licenses. It must come down to trust and
individual responsibility. But given the choice, would you rather
have doctors licensed by an agency whose income depends on its
reputation for honesty, or by a trade union of doctors granted a
government monopoly regardless of their performance?
While no one has yet offered any professional licensing services for
Oceania, Walter Smith, whose advertisement you will see on page 11,
has begun offering simple registrations of individuals, business, and
contracts. Though he is currently a monopoly, it wouldn't surprise me
if someone else got into the business and started offering more
services. That might prompt Walter to expand his services or change
his price structure, which might force the competing agency to find
more efficient ways to use its computer technology, for example, by
offering on-line queries of the registration database by modem from
the user's home. The possibilities are endless. Isn't competition
great?
