Subject: Mathematica is fairly stupid

>  One mistake I think AI people make in general is
> attempting to get computers to duplicate the highest functions the
> human brain is capable of without getting it to do the lowest ones.
> If dogs don't produce physics breakthroughs, I see no reason even a
> wildly successful AI system should.
> 
> Perry

The "bottom-up" approach suggested by these remarks has been adopted by 
the people doing (in my opinion) the most interesting AI/robotics work 
nowadays, like Rod Brooks at MIT.  Brooks builds little robot bugs that 
do very simple things (hiding from the light, picking up soda cans) 
based on even simpler behavioral rules.  I imagine that, with the right 
sort of environment control and adaptive strategies, such creatures 
could be made to evolve into something more interesting. 

To elaborate on Perry's comments, I think that the message is that we 
can't hope to build devices that short-circuit 60 million years of 
evolution; we can only hope to accelerate a simulated evolution by 
starting low, aiming high, and steering the process in the right 
direction.




