From: GRAPS@galileo.arc.nasa.gov
Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

Jay R. Freeman continues this thread with thoughtful
comments about:

>[The] Physics [field] has usually adopted an analytic/synthetic 
>approach to the discovery and application of physical laws ...

and he goes on to discuss how some approaches may not be best
at analyzing physical systems:

>given a complicated system to understand, what you do is take it
>to pieces, discover the laws that govern each piece separately,
>then add up (or whatever, with mutual interaction, etc.) the
>behaviors of the pieces to obtain the behavior of the whole.
>But suppose that doesn't always work.
...
>    I need some names; I will call phenomena of this sort
>"nondissectable", and others "dissectable". 

I have thought about this for a number of years, beginning when
I read Gleick's book: _Chaos_ in '87 (and subsequently pursued some
research modeling planetary ring systems as chaotic processes for my
job).  I think what you're describing is the difference between
analyzing LINEAR and NONLINEAR systems.

	dissectable --> LINEAR
	nondissectable --> NONLINEAR

Here's what I wrote in an introductory paper to chaos 5 years ago:

"An essential difference exists between linear and nonlinear systems.
Mathematically, linear equations are special in that any two solutions can 
be added together to form a new solution.  As a consequence,  analytic 
methods have been established for solving any linear system. You simply break 
up the complicated system into many simple pieces and patch together the 
separated solutions for each piece to form a solution to the full problem. In 
contrast,  two solutions to a nonlinear system cannot be added together to form
a new solution.  The system  must be treated in its full complexity.  No general
analytic approach exists for solving them.  Nonlinear equations can generate
either order or chaos and for those that generate chaotic motion, there are no
useful analytic solutions."

As you are probably aware, MANY people are working on analyzing nonlinear
systems using such mathematic tools as Lyaponov exponents, phase-space
reconstruction, wavelets and on and on.  I'm a little behind in keeping
up with the developments in the nonlinear dynamics field, but you get 
my point. Analyzing nonlinear systems is an important (growing) scientific
endeavor for exactly the reasons that you described in your message.

Amara Graps		AMARA on HEx
graps@gal.arc.nasa.gov


