Date: Fri, 05 Nov 1993 13:22:02 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Mathematica is fairly stupid

Nick Szabo writes:

> In other words, we need to make computers capable of trophic
> reactions and conditioning before we can get them to do
> arithmetic, logic, algorithms, math, accounting spreadsheets,
> filing, and the like.  I guess the computer industry is one big 
> mistake.  

No, it's not a mistake.  It just isn't making things that shed any light on the 
way that human brains work. 

> One big mistake AI critics make in general is assuming we have to
> mimic human biology to make computers do things humans can do.

That depends on what you mean by "what humans can do."  If you mean playing chess, 
well, sure -- there's no way that I'm going to beat a machine that can store all 
the possible board configurations for the next ten moves.  On the other hand, what 
the hell good is chess?  

I believe that the "one big mistake" is no mistake at all; rather, 
it is the reaction of people who watched a few decades of AI research (remember 
Roger Schank?  Frames and Scripts?  GPS?  SHRDLU?  And... gasp... ELIZA???) 
produce toy programs that could deal only with incredibly restricted domains like 
geometric solids and articles on terrorism.  

In order to get AI's that are worth a damn, we're going to have to mimic the kind 
of adaptive flexibility that only biological systems have been shown to exhibit.
As John Searle has pointed out, this behavior is only "computational" in the 
trivial sense that you can build a computational model of any physical phenomenon.
Of course, we could add superfast number-crunching and other extant technologies 
to whatever systems we develop, but they would only be tools for the systems to 
use, just as spreadsheets and calculators are for us.

-- Simon!



