Subject: No New Continents (was: Future Science)

[ ... continuing my own thread on dissectable/nondissectable systems,
  with a few thoughts on how the study of nonlinear systems interacts
  therewith ... ]

    There are many known physical systems which are difficult to "dissect"
(I've already created more new terminology than the subject probably warrants,
so I will try to avoid "quasi-nondissectable" and its kin -- besides, Klaus!
would no doubt satirize me if I did use them) because the effect becomes
small when the system becomes "small" or "simple".  One familiar example is
Newtonian gravitation, and the gist of my point here is that if we were
itty bitty creatures living on itty bitty worlds, it might be hard for us
to discover that gravity existed at all.  Newtonian gravitation is a nice
linear phenomenon, in that gravitational potentials are additive as scalars,
and gravitational forces are additive as vectors.  The difficulty of
dissection is because the gravitational force between two constant-density
spheres of radius R in contact goes as the product of their (equal) masses
divided by R-squared, but each mass goes as R-cubed, so the overall force
goes as R-to-the-fourth -- which diminishes *very* rapidly as you "dissect"
the system by making R small.  The gravitational acceleration on each
sphere -- which might be more important in many situations -- goes as R,
which still diminishes (ta-da) as R goes to zero.

    Caution, caution!  When I say "as R goes to zero", I do *not* mean that
I am holding the object size constant and bringing them closer and closer
together, I mean that I am scaling the *entire* *system* -- we start with
two 1-meter spheres 1 meter apart, then go to two 10-cm spheres 10 cm apart,
and so on ...

    In the context of Amara's recent comments, this example is interesting
because it comprises a linear system -- gravitational potentials are additive
-- which is nonetheless difficult to "dissect", or even to discover, if you
and the constituents of your world are all too small.

                                  -- Jay Freeman

