I can't see how you get opposite spins for "spin one-half" particles to be
90 degrees apart, except that the terms "spin one-half" and "spin one" 
might suggest that "one-half" is half that of a "one".  It's the other way
round; photons (spin one) are the ones where vertical polarization is
opposite to horizontal.  The thought experiment is with spin 1/2 particles
like an electron - positron pair, so 180 degrees apart is "opposite" and
135 is not equivalent to 45 degrees. 

>Also, I have a problem with Penrose's example of the EPR paradox
>(on page 284 of _Emperor_): He supposes that there are three settings
>for the E and P measurers, and then goes on to show that no combination of
>results would yield equal probabilities of agreement or disagreement.  But
>this is trivial, since there are 3x3=9 combinations, which surely cannot give
>equal numbers of agreements or disagreements.  How about the case of 4
>settings?

The example I gave (from a footnote at the end of Penrose's chapter on
QM) and the one you mention above are both versions of Bell's inequalities
provided by David Mermin with the 2x2 version being nearer to the Aspect
experiments.  

Try it with gradually ascending even numbers of settings, and the only
combination where you can get as many YES/NO agreements as disagreements is
where half the measurements (A, B, C, ....n)  are YESes and the other NOes
and the corresponding A', B', C', ...n' are divided 50-50 as well. All the
others have more disagreements than agreements.  But you could extend that
to a very large even number and then it only works if the results on one
of the particles just miraculously happened to give exactly half YESes and
half NOes.  My original description is probably best, though.

*In Search of Schrodinger's Cat* has another version of the Bell test,
around page 224.

>All your arguments from quantum theory, however, do not support Many Worlds
>over Copenhagen, they merely preclude local realistic models.  How does
>Aspect's experiment support one or the other?

That's right, the experiments of the 70s and 80s on proton and photon spin
falsified local realistic models and verified QM as a whole.  QM had
already been demonstrated in numerous experiments (you only need to look 
at the photos of double-slit experiments where the light was turned down
to see the evidence).  The more recent experiments to test Bell's
inequalities validated the EPR "paradox" as expected, which demonstrated
that all the implications of QM were correct.  So you had to choose from 
Copenhagenism, Many Worlds/Minds, or one of the lesser interpretations.
Copenhagenism required FTL signalling sprinkled with a dash of mysticism
(or a refusal to think about the implications); this didn't stop people
from believing in it.  It could still be true, it's possible that there
is a curious dichotomy between "observer" and "observed" together with 
Special Relativity violations / holistic links between parts of a
universe, but Many Minds works fine without such assumptions.
Falsification of Many Minds / Worlds or Copenhagenism would need quantum
computers.  Or for personal falsification you run an experiment consisting
of a series of n bombs, each with a p% chance of not going off, with
consequent immediate death if any do.  At the end, the copy of yourself
that survives and is now the richest man on the planet knows that either
a 1 in (100 / p) ^ n coincidence has occured, or the single universe
model is false.  I plan to wait for the quantum computers!

